Mark Odell[041025]modellus@pathlink.com[041025][041025]``I once asked John Hospers, the Libertarian Party's first presidential candidate (1972), if the realities of The System would not eventually corrupt Libertarian office holders, even if the Party did succeed in gaining the presidency. His answer was very straightforward and practical. He said that indeed that was a possibility, but that it might take fifty or a hundred years for the Party to become as corrupt as the Republicans and Democrats. In the meantime, not only would the people of this country enjoy fifty to a hundred years of drastically increased freedom and prosperity, but they would be buying considerable additional time in which to come up with alternatives to our present system before it collapses. I thought that was a very honest answer and certainly a worthwhile reason for Libertarian-Party members to continue to pursue their goal of gaining the presidency.''<BR>-- Robert J. Ringer, RESTORING THE AMERICAN DREAM, Chapter 9<BR><BR>Congratulations, Mr. Hospers; it only took you 32 years to become thus corrupted, and you didn't even have the bad excuse of actually having held elective office and succumbing to its temptations (sewer, hot tub, etc.).<BR><BR>The belief that voting for "the lesser of two evils" is okay just because "he's not as bad as the other guy" is precisely what got us into this mess in the first place. Now, is this ruse just supposed to go on until the end of time, incrementally grinding away at our rights until they're all ground away? When and where does it ever stop? When do our elites (the real enemy here, please note) ever actually lose anything at this game, if they can snow the majority of voters every time into "going along to get along"? (The problem with the "hold your nose and vote for the not-as-bad guy now, and we'll do something about it next election/wait for a better moment" strategy is that when "next time" arrives, the same arguments in favor of the next "not-as-bad" guy which worked so well last time will be dutifully trotted out. The problem with waiting for the better moment is that if our elites can keep getting you to vote for one of their two O&O sock-puppets, then they can prevent that better moment from ever arriving, and instead we get only a collection of progressively-worse moments.)<BR><BR>I for one will not be stampeded by apocalyptic warnings of doom and gloom -- especially ones put forth by individuals and groups with a vested interest in inducing me to think in terms of false alternatives. Thank you, no.<BR>[041025]10-29-2004 19:47
